MPC complete survey
===================

The Minor Planet Center orbit catalogue (MPCORB.DAT) treated as a survey
that has found every cold classical Kuiper belt object (free inclination
< 4 deg, Huang et al. 2022) brighter
than an absolute magnitude limit, anywhere on the sky. It is a constraint on
the bright end of the H distribution, where the pencil-beam surveys in this
repository have almost no objects.

The survey is in r: MPC.csv h is H_r (PS1/Rubin r scale), measured from the
MPC observations for the objects near and below the limit, and the limit is
H_r < 5.42 (MPC H < 5.5).

Files
-----

  make_mpc.py          MPCORB.DAT + MPC_photometry.csv -> MPC.csv, MPC.duplicates;
                       --completeness, --calibrate
  MPC.csv              every MPCORB orbit with 30 <= a <= 150 au and MPC H <= 7
                       (1917 orbits, 406 with comp = cold), snapshot 2026-10-19;
                       h = H_r, h_mpc = the MPC's H, ifree = free inclination
  mpc_photometry.py    MPC observations -> H_r (MPC_photometry.csv,
                       MPC_band_offsets.txt, obs/)
  obs/                 the MPC observation files (<desig>.txt) and their
                       reduction (<desig>_reduced.csv)
  MPC.duplicates       MPC designation and release name of the 140 objects that
                       are also OSSOS/CFEPS detections (survey_debias_joint)
  MPC.vetting          resonance vetting of the cold candidates
  MPC.toml             the complete-survey spec read by survey_debias
  MPC_completeness.*   discovery year against H_r (--completeness)
  MPC_calibration.txt  MPC H and H_r minus OSSOS/CFEPS Hsur (--calibrate)
  vet_resonances.py    REBOUND integrations behind MPC.vetting
  make_pointings.py    writes pointings.list and MPC_<lon>.eff (the ossssim version)
  pointings.list       36 ecliptic-band tiles, each at its own opposition date
  MPC_<lon>.eff        filter= r; per tile, magnitude limits per rate range that
                       imitate H_r < h_lim at opposition
  validate_ossssim.py  runs the cold model through pointings.list + MPC_*.eff
  MPC_ossssim_validation.{txt,png}   its result
  mpcread.py           the original extraction script (Python 2, ephem); kept
                       for reference, superseded by make_mpc.py

  MPCORB.DAT and the Bernardinelli et al. (2020, ApJS 247, 32) epub are not
  committed. To rebuild, download MPCORB.DAT from
  https://www.minorplanetcenter.org/iau/MPCORB/MPCORB.DAT, then

    python make_mpc.py            # orbits; h from the existing MPC_photometry.csv
    python mpc_photometry.py      # (--refresh) re-measure H_r of the candidates
    python make_mpc.py            # h from the new photometry
    python make_mpc.py --completeness; python make_mpc.py --calibrate

  and record the snapshot date in MPC.toml.

How h was put on the r scale
----------------------------

MPCORB gives one H per object (kept as h_mpc), computed by the MPC from
whatever bands were reported, with its own band corrections; it is close to
V but not on any defined system. MPC.csv h is H_r instead:

1. mpc_photometry.py measures H_r for every cold candidate with MPC H < 6.0
   (40 objects; 36 have usable photometry). For each it downloads the
   observations from the MPC (show_object?object_id=ID, which creates and
   links the 80-column file tmp2/ID.txt; cached in obs/), computes r, Delta
   and the phase angle alpha of each observation from the MPC.csv orbit
   (two-body heliocentric with light time, Earth from astropy; the predicted
   positions are within 1-5 arcmin of the observed ones over 1997-2026), and
   reduces each CCD magnitude in its reported band X to

       H_X = m - 5 log10(r Delta) - beta alpha,   beta = 0.14 mag/deg (alpha <= 1.4 deg)

2. The band offsets X - r are measured from the objects themselves
   (MPC_band_offsets.txt): Rubin (X05) r is the reference, and each band of a
   system is offset by the median, over the objects observed in both, of its
   median H_X minus the Rubin r one (16 objects have Rubin r):

       g@Rubin +0.86   i@Rubin -0.41   z@Rubin -0.63
       w@PS1   -0.04   G@PS1   -0.06   i@PS1   -0.48   r@PS1  -0.28

   Pan-STARRS (F51, F52) "G" magnitudes are on the r/w scale (not g, not Gaia
   G). The cold colours in colour.toml (r - i = 0.6, r - z = 1.1, w - r = -0.2)
   are too red for these objects, so they are not used here.

3. H_r of an object is the median over its nights (night medians per band
   and observatory first) of H_X - (X - r), from PS1 and Rubin only; older R/V
   reports from other observatories scatter by 0.2-0.4 mag and are not used.
   The bands of an object agree to ~0.05 mag. Error: 1.25 x robust sd /
   sqrt(n nights); beta = 0.08 or 0.20 instead of 0.14 moves H_r by ~0.03.

4. make_mpc.py sets h = H_r (h_source = photometry, h_err its error) for the
   measured objects, and h = h_mpc - 0.084 for the others (h_source =
   mpc_offset, h_err = 0.082): 0.084 is the median MPC H - H_r of the 35
   measured objects whose photometry agrees with their MPC H, 0.082 the
   robust sd. (420356) Praamzius is the exception: its PS1 magnitudes (G ~
   23.4-23.9) and its older R reports (~21.5) disagree by 2 mag, so its
   measured H_r (7.23, 1.48 mag fainter than MPC H) is not used
   (h_source = mpc_offset_photometry_rejected; the threshold is 0.5 mag).

For comparison (MPC_calibration.txt), the OSSOS/CFEPS release Hsur of the
cold objects in both is brighter than this H_r by 0.11 (r, 23 objects) and
than MPC H by 0.20: the OSSOS/CFEPS and PS1/Rubin H scales differ by ~0.1 mag.

The completeness limit
----------------------

Bernardinelli et al. (2020) do not give an H completeness limit for the MPC.
They show that DES Y4 is complete to m_r ~ 23.3 inside the DES footprint and
recovers every known MPC object brighter than that. The limit used here is
measured from the MPC itself: if the census is complete at some H, nothing
brighter has been discovered recently (MPC_completeness.txt, .png; the
discovery year is the provisional-designation year).

  * Cold sample (comp = cold, i_free < 4 deg), in H_r (bins with 5.42 as an
    edge): 5 objects below 5.42, none found since 2014; 2 of the 8 in
    5.42-5.67 were found since 2017 (2021 CJ46, 2025 NH497). h_lim = 5.42.
    (With the earlier ecliptic cut, i < 5 deg, the same limit held: 9 objects
    below 5.42, none found since 2014.)
  * All TNOs (30 < a < 150 au): 31 discoveries brighter than MPC H = 5.5 since
    2017, nearly all on high-inclination or large-a, high-e orbits (scattering
    and detached objects found far from the ecliptic). The MPC is not complete
    at that H for the whole Kuiper belt, only for the low-inclination
    classical belt.
  * The cold objects just below the limit were mostly found in 2011-2014:
    completeness at this level is recent (Pan-STARRS, OSSOS, DES).
  * 2025 ME398 (MPC H = 5.45, a = 41.66 au, i = 3.5 deg) is a one-opposition
    orbit with a 21 day arc (uncertainty 9). Its a puts it inside the inner
    edge of the cold components (42.4 au), so it is not in the sample; if a
    longer arc moves it into 42.4-47.7 au it is a new cold object near the
    limit and h_lim must come down.

The sample: cold = free inclination < 4 deg
-------------------------------------------

comp = cold when cl = cla, p = m, 42.4 <= a <= 47.7 au and i_free < 4 deg.
The free inclination is that of Huang, Gladman & Volk (2022, ApJS 259, 54):
the angle between the orbit pole and the local forcing pole, which a
non-resonant orbit keeps (to < 1 deg over 4 Gyr) while its ecliptic i
cycles. I_free < 4 deg separates the cold classicals from the hot
population much better than ecliptic i < 5 deg (their Figs 1 and 6).
survey_debias.ifree supplies it:

  * ifree_source = huang2022_table (651 rows): Huang et al. Table 1 (I_free
    from their double-averaged Hamiltonian, barycentric elements), looked up
    by number or provisional designation;
  * ifree_source = huang2022_grid (519 rows): their forcing-pole grid (Table
    2, nearest grid point, ~0.1 deg) applied to the MPCORB elements after
    converting them to barycentric at the MPCORB epoch. On Huang's own 1332
    classicals the grid reproduces Table 1 to < 0.1 deg for 98% and gives
    the same side of 4 deg for 99.85%;
  * rows outside the main belt (a < 39.4 or > 47.7 au) have no I_free.

cl = cla needs 39.4 <= a <= 47.7 au and q >= 37 au. For the objects in Huang
et al. Table 1 their own dynamical classification (huang_reso: 0 classical,
-1 scattering, pq resonant; a manual 10 Myr classification) decides cl;
otherwise MPC.vetting (vet_resonances.py) does. The a >= 42.4 au limit is
the inner edge of the OSSOS 1.0 cold components, which the efficiency is
computed from; Huang et al. find no classical with I_free < 4 deg inside
~42 au (the nu_8 and nu_18 secular resonances).

With h_lim = 5.42 the sample is 5 objects, all multi-opposition, all in
Huang et al. Table 1 and all with measured H_r:

   H_r   err  MPC H  object                   a      e      i   i_free  found
   5.15  0.02  5.23  (523745) 2014 TD86    43.29  0.006  1.69   0.83   2014
   5.20  0.02  5.25  (523721) 2014 LR28    43.85  0.057  1.47   1.68   2014
   5.20  0.02  5.26  (79360) Sila-Nunam    44.08  0.015  2.24   3.79   1997
   5.37  0.05  5.55  2011 LJ29             43.94  0.081  0.24   1.76   2011
   5.40  0.03  5.53  (534625) 2014 UQ224   44.72  0.045  1.95   2.16   2014

Compared with the ecliptic cut (i < 5 deg; 9 objects), (556416) 2014 OE394,
(523724) 2014 MA70 and (523678) 2013 XB26 are hot (i_free = 5.38, 5.51,
5.19 deg although i < 4.3 deg), and (523588) 2000 CN105 is a 9:5 resonant
in Huang et al. (our own 10 Myr vetting called it non-resonant: one of its
clones librated in the 9:5 in a short test; Huang et al. count an object as
resonant if its angle stops circulating at any time). None of the five is an
OSSOS or CFEPS detection, so no release detection is dropped as a duplicate.

MPC.toml sigma_h = 0.05 is the scatter of these H_r about the true H_r
(median-of-nights errors 0.01-0.05, phase coefficient ~0.03).

Resonance vetting (MPC.vetting)
-------------------------------

vet_resonances.py integrates the 40 candidates with MPC H <= 6.0 (selected,
at the time, by ecliptic i < 5 deg) for 10 Myr (WHFast, 0.5 yr step, Sun +
giant planets from JPL Horizons at the MPCORB epoch 2026-06-09), each with
clones at a -/+ 0.005 au, and tests every Neptune resonance p:q (p <= 40,
order <= 6) within 0.4 au of the mean a for libration (the resonant angle
avoids a 20 deg arc) in ten 1 Myr windows. An object is resonant when the
nominal orbit librates in at least half of them. It now selects candidates by
i_free < 4 deg; every cold candidate with MPC H <= 6 is either in Huang et
al. Table 1 or in MPC.vetting, so no new integrations were needed.

None of the 40 is resonant by this criterion, and nominal and clones agree
for all. Two librate part of the time: (66652) Borasisi in the 7:4 (3 windows
in 10, H_r = 5.74) and 2026 EK129 near the 2:1 (one opposition, MPC H =
5.88). Both are above h_lim.

The ossssim version
-------------------

ossssim efficiencies depend on apparent magnitude and rate, never on H.
make_pointings.py imitates "H_r < h_lim everywhere" with 36 polygon tiles of
10 deg in ecliptic longitude (|beta| <= 8 deg, 1 deg overlap between
neighbours; tiles crossing RA = 0 are written twice, at RA0 and RA0 + 360),
each observed from the geocentre on the date its centre is at opposition, in
2026. Each tile has its own MPC_<lon>.eff (filter= r) with Earth's distance
and tangential speed on that date: at opposition the rate fixes the
distance, so ten rate ranges with m0 = M0 + alpha_rate * rate (a narrow
single tanh, width 0.05 mag) follow

    m_lim(r) = h_lim + 5 log10(r (r - R_E)) + phase,

phase = 0.033 mag being ossssim's H-G (G = 0.15) term at the small phase
angles inside a tile. No tracking loss and essentially no photometric noise
(mag_error= 0.001 0 99 0 99 0: an exactly zero first coefficient gives NaN
measured magnitudes fainter than m = 21 in the ossssim build of 2026-10-20);
the m0 curve is within 0.005 mag of m_lim over 37-62 au.

validate_ossssim.py (40000 cold model rows with i_free < 4 deg, H_r uniform
in 5.42 +/- 0.75; MPC_ossssim_validation.txt):

  detected for H_r < 5.27:  1.0000        detected for H_r > 5.57:  0.0000
  50% edge at H_r = 5.415 (-0.005)        90% -> 10% between 5.395 and 5.495

A first version with one .eff for the whole year (Earth's mean speed, no
phase term) had its edge 0.04 mag bright and was 0.2 mag wide: Earth's speed
changes by 1.7% over the year, which moves the inferred distance by ~0.9 au.

To use it as a constraint in a SurveySimulator run, simulate the model
through characterization/MPC and compare the detected cold objects (i_free <
4 deg, 42.4 <= a <= 47.7 au) with MPC.csv (comp = cold, h < 5.42). The tiles
only reach |beta| <= 8 deg: fine for i_free < 4 deg orbits (ecliptic i <~ 6
deg, |beta| <~ 6.3 deg at opposition), not for other populations
(make_pointings.py --beta-max). The
ossssim version applies no H scatter; survey_debias adds sigma_h
analytically.

survey_debias
-------------

survey_debias does not run the tiles: a complete survey's efficiency is
analytic, eps(H_r) = f_cut * 1(H_r < h_lim), f_cut being the fraction of the
model's cold population inside the sample cut (0.714 for i_free < 4 deg,
the model rows' free inclinations computed with survey_debias.ifree):

  survey_debias_eff --root . --complete characterization/MPC/MPC.toml \
      --model ../KuiperBeltModels/OSSOS/Classical-ModelUsed.dat \
      --tag coldfree --ifree-max 4 [--overlap-with OSSOS_v11 ...]

writes characterization/MPC/eps_coldfree.csv (and, with --overlap-with, the
overlap tables in the model band, for inspection; survey_debias_joint
computes them itself). The joint configuration joint/coldfree_all_mpc.toml
adds the MPC as the first survey in priority (joint/cold_all_mpc.toml is the
earlier i < 5 deg version, eps_cold.csv with f_cut 0.758); see the joint fit
notes in survey_debias/docs/joint_fit/README.md.

Result with i_free < 4 deg (joint/coldfree_all*.toml; joint/mpc_compare.py
--config coldfree_all_mpc.toml ...): the all-survey fit without the MPC
predicts 9.1 +5.5 -3.7 cold MPC objects below H_r = 5.42, 5 are found
(P(N <= 5) = 0.22); adding them gives N(H_r < 5) = 1.0 +0.7 -0.4 (2.2 +2.2
-1.2 without) and N(H_r < 6) = 77 +16 -13 (94 +29 -24), and alpha = 0.23 +/-
0.04 with the small-alpha side of the profile (alpha < 0.13) removed.

Result of the first version (MPC H < 5.5 on the M scale, M - r = +0.20,
7 objects, i < 5 deg; joint/mpc_compare.py): the all-survey cold fit without the MPC
predicted 10.3 +5.9 -4.6 MPC objects below the limit, 7 were found; adding
them narrowed N(H_r < 5) from 2.3 +2.5 -1.5 to 2.0 +1.4 -0.8 and N(H_r < 6)
from 109 +/- 33 to 102 +/- 18, and cut the alpha < 0.13 side of the
profile. The fits have not been rerun on the r-band sample.
